import type { SpeakingInterviewPack } from './speaking-model.js';

const pack = (topic: string, band: number, pairs: [string, string][]): SpeakingInterviewPack => ({
  topic, band, questions: pairs.map(([prompt, answer]) => ({ prompt, answer })),
});

/** Practice-target bands are estimates; these examples are not examiner-rated scripts. */
export const additionalSpeakingPart3Packs: SpeakingInterviewPack[] = [
  pack('Consumer privacy and digital services', 6.5, [
    ['Why do people share personal information with online companies?', 'Often they want a convenient service and do not see the exchange clearly. A shopping app may request location access during setup, when the customer is focused on placing an order. People should receive a simple explanation of what is necessary, rather than a long notice they are unlikely to read.'],
    ['Should companies collect information that is not needed to deliver their service?', 'Only with genuine, informed permission, in my view. Knowing a delivery address is necessary to deliver a parcel, but tracking every other place the customer visits serves a different purpose. Refusing that extra collection should not prevent someone from using the ordinary service they have already paid for.'],
    ['How can public authorities help people protect their privacy?', 'They can require understandable choices and penalise companies that misuse information. Education is useful, but individuals cannot inspect every technical process behind an app. Clear limits on collection and independent enforcement would reduce that burden, especially for people who have little time or technical knowledge to investigate each service.'],
    ['Is a personalised service always worth the loss of privacy?', 'No, because the benefit may be quite small while the information reveals a great deal. A music recommendation might be helpful, but continuous location tracking could expose sensitive routines. People need a choice based on the actual exchange, and they should be able to change their mind later.'],
    ['Why can deleting an online account be more difficult than creating one?', 'A business has an incentive to gain customers and keep their information, so registration is often made easier than leaving. That imbalance is unfair when several confusing steps are deliberately added. Deletion should still confirm identity to prevent malicious requests, but it should not become a test of the customer’s persistence.'],
  ]),
  pack('Product durability and repair', 5.5, [
    ['Why do some people replace a broken product rather than repair it?', 'Repair can be expensive or difficult to arrange, while a new product is easy to buy. If a technician cannot obtain a small spare part, replacement may seem like the only option. So the decision is not always careless consumption; the repair system itself may make a sensible choice impractical.'],
    ['What could make household repair services more accessible?', 'Clear prices and local collection points would help people know what to expect. Manufacturers should also provide parts and basic technical information to qualified repairers. A low income household may need support with an initial repair bill, because a cheaper outcome over several years can still be unaffordable today.'],
    ['Should schools teach children how to maintain everyday objects?', 'Yes, simple maintenance can build confidence and reduce waste. Children could learn to mend clothing or care for a bicycle, with proper supervision. They should also learn where the safety boundary lies, because encouraging practical skills does not mean asking them to open electrical equipment or handle dangerous batteries.'],
    ['Can a long product guarantee encourage better design?', 'It can give the manufacturer a financial reason to prevent common faults. However, the guarantee must be easy to use and cover realistic problems, rather than exclude almost everything in small print. Otherwise, the promise looks impressive but does little to change either product quality or the customer’s experience.'],
    ['How should buyers compare cheap products with durable ones?', 'They should consider the expected years of use, repair options and running costs, not just the purchase price. But it is important to recognise that some people cannot afford the durable option immediately. Installment support or reliable secondhand markets can help, provided the arrangement does not create expensive debt.'],
  ]),
  pack('Wild animals in urban neighbourhoods', 6, [
    ['Why do wild animals sometimes move into cities?', 'Cities offer food, shelter and warmth, while development may reduce natural habitat nearby. An animal can learn that bins provide a regular meal. The reason varies by species, though, so a rise in sightings does not automatically mean that the whole local ecosystem is healthier or more damaged.'],
    ['How should cities respond when residents are worried about wildlife?', 'They should explain the actual risk and offer practical advice, such as securing waste or keeping a safe distance. Trained staff can handle genuinely dangerous situations. Treating every unfamiliar animal as a threat would be unnecessary, but dismissing residents’ concerns could make people take unsafe action on their own.'],
    ['Is feeding wild animals in public places a good idea?', 'Usually it creates problems because animals become dependent or gather where conflict is likely. Bread given to birds can also be unsuitable food. People often feed them with kind intentions, so clear explanations are more useful than simply scolding them. Suitable habitat provides a better form of support.'],
    ['Can urban building design help wildlife?', 'Yes, some designs reduce avoidable harm, such as glass treatments that make windows visible to birds. Connected green spaces can also allow animals to move without crossing busy roads. The details need specialist advice, because a feature advertised as wildlife friendly may do little for the species actually living nearby.'],
    ['Should children learn about local wildlife as well as rare animals?', 'Definitely, because familiar species help children observe what happens around them. Studying insects in a school garden can reveal connections between plants, soil and food. Rare animals are interesting too, but focusing only on distant endangered species may leave pupils unaware of changes in the environment they can actually influence.'],
  ]),
  pack('Preparedness for natural disasters', 7, [
    ['Why do communities sometimes ignore disaster warnings?', 'Warnings may be frequent but unclear, or previous events may have been less serious than predicted. People also have practical concerns, such as protecting a business or leaving a pet behind. A warning is more effective when it explains the specific risk and gives a realistic route to safety.'],
    ['What makes an emergency evacuation plan effective?', 'People need to know where to go, how to get there and who can help if they lack transport. Practice can reveal obstacles that a document misses, such as an inaccessible exit. The plan must also account for visitors and people with limited language ability, rather than assume every resident receives the same information.'],
    ['Should governments focus more on prevention than emergency response?', 'Prevention deserves greater attention because good drainage or stronger buildings can reduce harm before an event occurs. However, not every danger can be removed, so response capacity remains necessary. The sensible balance depends on local risks, and maintenance matters as much as announcing a new protective project.'],
    ['How can neighbours support vulnerable residents during emergencies?', 'They can agree beforehand who may need a check or assistance, with the person’s permission. For example, a resident using a wheelchair might need help reaching an accessible pickup point. Volunteers require a clear link to emergency services so that good intentions do not lead them into an unsafe situation.'],
    ['Why is communication important after a disaster as well as before it?', 'People need reliable information about safe water, temporary housing and when returning home is possible. Conflicting rumours can send families back into danger or cause unnecessary panic. Regular updates should explain what is still uncertain, because admitting that a road has not yet been inspected is better than offering false reassurance.'],
  ]),
  pack('Preventing food waste', 4.5, [
    ['Why do households throw away food that could still be eaten?', 'They may buy more than they need or forget what is already in the fridge. Date labels can also be confusing. A family might throw away an unopened product because they misunderstand the label, so better information and ordinary meal planning can help more than simply telling people to feel guilty.'],
    ['Should supermarkets sell imperfect fruit and vegetables?', 'Yes, if the food is safe and its quality is explained honestly. A curved carrot can still make a good meal. Selling these products at a lower price may help customers and growers, although a shop should not use the idea to sell damaged food that is no longer suitable.'],
    ['How can restaurants reduce food left on customers’ plates?', 'They could offer different portion sizes and describe the amount clearly. Someone with a smaller appetite would then pay for what they actually want. Taking leftovers home can help too, but containers and food safety need attention. Reducing waste should not mean pressuring customers to finish more than they need.'],
    ['Can sharing unwanted food help a community?', 'It can, especially when a local fridge accepts safe, clearly labelled items. Neighbours may use food that would otherwise be discarded. However, donations are unpredictable, so this cannot replace dependable help for people who cannot afford meals. Someone must also check storage conditions and remove unsuitable items regularly.'],
    ['What should children learn about wasting food?', 'They can learn how food is grown, stored and used in simple meals. Seeing the work involved makes waste easier to understand. Adults should not frighten them or force them to eat too much; learning suitable portions and how to save leftovers is a more practical lesson.'],
  ]),
  pack('Fashion choices and textile waste', 6.5, [
    ['Why can inexpensive clothing lead to more waste?', 'A low price makes replacement seem easier than repair, particularly when styles change quickly. Some garments are also made in ways that do not survive repeated washing. The problem is not that affordable clothes are wrong, but that short life and constant promotion can turn them into disposable products.'],
    ['What might encourage people to keep clothing longer?', 'Good care instructions, accessible repairs and designs that remain useful across several seasons would help. Someone may gladly repair a favourite coat if a zip can be replaced affordably. Brands should support that choice rather than promote a new purchase as the answer to every small fault or change in fashion.'],
    ['Are secondhand clothes likely to become more acceptable?', 'I think so, because many people now see them as a way to find something distinctive and save money. Online platforms have made access easier. Quality checks and clear descriptions still matter, though, since buying a poorly described item and returning it repeatedly can undermine both convenience and environmental benefits.'],
    ['Should clothing companies be responsible for products after they are sold?', 'They should share responsibility, because design determines how easily a garment can be repaired or recycled. A collection programme is useful only if the items genuinely find another use. Companies need to report what happens to collected clothing rather than use a donation box mainly to encourage the next purchase.'],
    ['How can fashion allow self expression without constant consumption?', 'People can combine familiar items, alter clothing or exchange pieces with friends. Creativity does not require every look to be entirely new. That approach is easier when social expectations value individual style rather than the latest brand, so media and influential figures can help by showing repeated use instead of endless new purchases.'],
  ]),
  pack('Work organised through digital platforms', 7.5, [
    ['What attracts workers to app based jobs?', 'The promise of choosing working hours can be appealing, especially for someone combining jobs or care responsibilities. Entry may also be relatively quick. However, apparent flexibility can be limited by demand peaks or reward systems, so workers need to understand actual earnings and conditions rather than only the recruitment message.'],
    ['How can customer ratings affect platform workers?', 'Ratings can help identify poor service, but a low score may reflect a delay or problem outside the worker’s control. If access to work depends heavily on the score, workers may accept unreasonable demands. An appeal process and clear separation of responsibility are essential before ratings influence someone’s livelihood.'],
    ['Should platforms reveal how they allocate work?', 'They should explain the rules that materially affect earnings and access, even if every technical detail remains private. A driver needs to know whether rejecting an unsafe journey leads to fewer future offers. Without that information, workers cannot make informed choices or challenge unfair treatment in a meaningful way.'],
    ['What protection is important for delivery workers?', 'Accident cover, realistic deadlines and predictable minimum earnings would address major risks. Riders should not have to recover restaurant delays by travelling dangerously. The rules also need to work in practice: a safety policy means little if a hidden performance system still penalises everyone who prioritises a careful journey.'],
    ['Can platform work be a stable long term career?', 'It can be for some people, but stability depends on conditions rather than the app itself. Demand, fees and access rules may change suddenly. Opportunities to build skills and savings are important, and workers should have clear information about these risks before relying on the work as their household’s main income.'],
  ]),
  pack('Public toilets and access to city life', 5, [
    ['Why are public toilets important in a town centre?', 'They allow people to stay outside for work, shopping or leisure without worrying about a basic need. This is especially important for older residents and people with certain medical conditions. A town may look welcoming, but some people will avoid it if the only available toilet requires buying something.'],
    ['Why do some public toilets fall into poor condition?', 'Construction gets attention, while cleaning and repairs need a continuing budget. If nobody has clear responsibility, a broken lock or blocked sink may remain for weeks. Users then lose trust in the facility. Planning should include daily operation from the beginning, rather than treat it as a minor cost after opening.'],
    ['Should shops be asked to provide toilets for noncustomers?', 'A voluntary agreement can help, provided the shop receives suitable support and access is clearly marked. But businesses should not become the only solution, because opening hours and layouts vary. Councils still need dependable public provision, especially where a shop’s stairs make its toilet inaccessible to some people.'],
    ['What makes a public facility genuinely accessible?', 'Access involves more than putting a symbol on the door. The route, entrance, internal space and controls must work for different users, and the facility must actually be open. Consultation with people who use mobility aids can reveal practical problems that a designer might overlook during a brief inspection.'],
    ['How can technology improve information about public facilities?', 'A map showing locations and current opening hours can help people plan a journey. It should include accessibility details rather than simply list a toilet nearby. However, information is only useful when updated, and there must be another way to find it for people without a suitable phone.'],
  ]),
  pack('Water use during drought', 6, [
    ['Why is it difficult to reduce water use during a drought?', 'People may not know which activities use the most water, and some waste comes from leaks they cannot see. Habits also feel harmless when considered one household at a time. Clear information helps, but suppliers must repair their own systems too, otherwise residents may reasonably question the fairness of restrictions.'],
    ['Should the price of water rise when it is scarce?', 'A higher charge for excessive use can encourage conservation, but basic needs must remain affordable. A large family should not be treated exactly like a small household watering a decorative lawn. A fair system would account for essential use and medical needs while charging more for genuinely discretionary consumption.'],
    ['How can farmers adapt to reduced water availability?', 'They may change crops, improve irrigation or use soil practices that retain moisture. These changes can require money and time, so advice alone may be insufficient. Support should help farmers earn a living within sustainable limits rather than pay for equipment while continuing to encourage unlimited extraction from a shrinking reserve.'],
    ['What role should schools play in water conservation?', 'Schools can make the subject concrete by checking leaks or comparing how different taps operate. Pupils see that conservation is a practical task, not just a slogan. Facilities must support the lesson, though; children are unlikely to take the message seriously if a broken tap runs continuously outside their classroom.'],
    ['Can restrictions change water habits permanently?', 'They can introduce new routines, but habits may return when the immediate crisis ends. Lasting change is more likely when efficient equipment and suitable gardens make conservation easy. Authorities should explain the wider supply situation as well, so people understand that a rainy week does not necessarily restore underground reserves.'],
  ]),
  pack('Protecting coastlines from erosion', 7, [
    ['Why do people continue building homes near vulnerable coasts?', 'The location offers attractive views and access to leisure, while the risk may feel distant or uncertain. Buyers may also assume the government will eventually protect every property. Clear risk information and planning rules are important because a private choice can later create expensive demands for public defences.'],
    ['Should every eroding coastline be defended with a seawall?', 'Not necessarily. A wall may protect one section while changing erosion elsewhere, and maintenance can be costly. Authorities need to compare the long term effects with alternatives, including restoring natural buffers or relocating vulnerable uses. The decision is difficult, but promising protection everywhere can leave communities with unrealistic expectations.'],
    ['How can coastal communities participate in protection decisions?', 'They should receive understandable evidence and enough time to discuss options before a plan is fixed. Residents know how local access and livelihoods work, which may be missing from a technical model. Participation does not remove scientific uncertainty, but it can expose practical costs and make difficult trade offs more transparent.'],
    ['What support is fair for families asked to relocate?', 'Families need enough assistance to find a suitable home and maintain essential connections, not merely a warning to leave. Property compensation, moving costs and access to schools or work all matter. Support should follow clear criteria, while avoiding incentives for new construction in places already known to be unsafe.'],
    ['Can restoring natural coastal habitats reduce damage?', 'In some locations, dunes, wetlands or vegetation can absorb part of the force of waves and provide other ecological benefits. Their suitability depends on local conditions, and they are not a guarantee against every storm. Specialist assessment and continuing care are needed rather than presenting nature as a free, permanent defence.'],
  ]),
  pack('Security for tenants in rented homes', 6.5, [
    ['Why does a secure tenancy matter to a family?', 'It lets people plan schooling, work and everyday relationships without constantly expecting another move. Repeated relocation costs money and time even when each home is physically adequate. Security is therefore about more than comfort; it affects whether a family can build savings and rely on a stable local support network.'],
    ['How can rental rules balance tenants and property owners?', 'Rules should give tenants predictable notice and protection against arbitrary action, while owners retain a lawful way to address serious breaches or genuine needs. Clear procedures reduce uncertainty for both sides. A balance is harder to achieve if either party must rely on expensive disputes to discover what a contract really means.'],
    ['Is building more homes enough to solve rental insecurity?', 'It helps address shortages, but supply alone does not guarantee fair contracts or affordable options. New homes may target only high income renters. Governments also need to consider tenancy terms, assistance and location, because a cheaper home far from work can leave a household with substantial transport costs.'],
    ['What difficulties can frequent moves create for children?', 'Changing schools can interrupt lessons and friendships, and the child may have little control over the decision. A longer journey may also reduce participation in activities. Schools should help new pupils settle, but preventing avoidable instability through housing policy is better than repeatedly asking children to adapt to disruption.'],
    ['Should rental homes meet minimum quality standards?', 'Yes, because people under pressure to find accommodation may accept conditions that damage health or safety. Standards should cover essentials such as heating, sanitation and structural safety. Enforcement must also protect tenants from losing their home without an alternative, or reporting a serious fault could place them in a worse situation.'],
  ]),
  pack('Responsible ownership of companion animals', 4, [
    ['Why do people choose to keep pets?', 'Animals can provide company and a daily routine, especially for someone who lives alone. Families may also enjoy caring for an animal together. However, wanting companionship is only the beginning; people need to consider time, cost and the animal’s needs before deciding that a pet will suit their life.'],
    ['What should people learn before adopting an animal?', 'They should know about food, exercise, veterinary care and the likely length of the commitment. A puppy’s needs are quite different from those of a quiet older cat. Meeting an experienced carer can help them make a realistic decision rather than choose only from an appealing photograph.'],
    ['Should cities provide places where dogs can exercise?', 'Yes, appropriate spaces can improve animal welfare and reduce conflict in ordinary parks. Owners still need to supervise their dogs and respect people who are frightened or have allergies. The area should be safely designed and maintained, because simply naming an unused piece of land does not make it suitable.'],
    ['How can communities reduce the number of abandoned pets?', 'Clear information before adoption, affordable basic veterinary care and responsible breeding rules can help. Some owners abandon animals because circumstances change and they cannot find assistance. A trusted route to rehoming is important, although it should not encourage people to treat an animal as an easily replaceable purchase.'],
    ['Can children benefit from helping to care for a pet?', 'They can learn patience and notice another living creature’s needs, but adults must remain responsible. A child may enjoy feeding an animal yet forget during a busy week. The lesson is more useful when care is supervised and reliable, rather than letting the animal suffer to teach responsibility.'],
  ]),
  pack('Checking the reliability of news', 7.5, [
    ['Why does misleading news spread quickly?', 'A surprising or emotional claim encourages people to react before checking it. Sharing can also signal loyalty to a group, so the message becomes socially rewarding. Corrections often travel more slowly because they are less dramatic. That makes careful habits important even when the person sharing the story has no intention to mislead.'],
    ['What can readers do before sharing a striking claim?', 'They can look for the original source, check the date and compare reporting from credible outlets. A photograph may be genuine but attached to an unrelated event. Readers should also separate what is established from what is being inferred, rather than assume that a confident headline contains all the necessary evidence.'],
    ['Should news organisations explain how a story was verified?', 'Yes, especially for disputed claims, because visible methods help readers judge the evidence rather than trust a brand blindly. Explaining which records or witnesses were checked can be useful. Sources sometimes need protection, though, so transparency should describe the process without automatically exposing someone who could face harm.'],
    ['Can fact checking alone solve misinformation?', 'It is necessary but insufficient. A correction may not reach the people who saw the original claim, and some will distrust it before reading. Platform design, media education and reliable local reporting also matter. The aim should be to improve the information environment, not merely publish a response after each falsehood.'],
    ['How should schools teach students to assess news?', 'They could compare several reports of the same event and trace the evidence behind particular statements. That shows why wording, selection and missing context matter. Lessons should avoid telling students that one political viewpoint is always reliable, because the useful skill is evaluating a claim consistently across different sources.'],
  ]),
  pack('Online influence and spending decisions', 5.5, [
    ['Why can an influencer’s recommendation feel persuasive?', 'Followers may see the person regularly and feel that they know them, so a recommendation resembles friendly advice. But the relationship is different when payment is involved. Clear advertising labels help people recognise that a product’s appearance in an ordinary looking video may be a planned commercial message.'],
    ['Should influencers be responsible for checking products they promote?', 'They should make reasonable checks and avoid claims they cannot support, especially about health or money. A large audience gives their words real consequences. They cannot test every detail like a regulator, but that limitation is a reason to be cautious, not an excuse to repeat whatever a sponsor provides.'],
    ['How can social comparison affect buying habits?', 'People see selected possessions without seeing the owner’s debts or other costs. An expensive item may then appear normal or necessary for acceptance. The pressure can lead someone to spend beyond their means. Remembering that an online image is incomplete helps, though platforms also have a role in making advertising clearer.'],
    ['Can online recommendations benefit small businesses?', 'Certainly, a thoughtful review can introduce a good product to customers beyond the local area. This may be more affordable than conventional advertising. The relationship should remain transparent, and a sudden increase in demand needs planning; attention is not always helpful if the business cannot deliver what viewers expect.'],
    ['What should young consumers learn about sponsored content?', 'They need to recognise payment, gifts and sales commissions, not just a clear advertisement at the start of a programme. Comparing several sources and considering whether the product meets a real need are practical habits. The aim is informed choice, rather than assuming every recommendation is dishonest or every purchase is wasteful.'],
  ]),
  pack('Resolving disputes between neighbours', 6, [
    ['Why can small disagreements between neighbours become serious?', 'People may experience the same problem repeatedly, such as late noise, while the other person does not realise its effect. Assumptions about motive then grow. A simple conversation can help early, but it needs to be safe and respectful; telling someone to confront a threatening neighbour alone is not a sensible solution.'],
    ['What role can mediation play in a local dispute?', 'A neutral person can help each side explain the problem without immediately arguing about blame. They may identify a practical agreement, such as different times for noisy work. Mediation is less suitable where there is intimidation or a serious legal issue, so communities need other ways to obtain protection and formal advice.'],
    ['Should neighbourhood rules be very detailed?', 'Some clear rules are useful for shared spaces, noise and maintenance, but excessive detail can create unnecessary conflict. Residents need to understand the purpose and apply rules consistently. A process for discussing new situations is often more valuable than trying to predict every minor behaviour in a long document.'],
    ['How can shared activities improve relationships between neighbours?', 'A garden project or a small street event gives people a reason to meet before a problem occurs. Knowing someone makes it easier to ask a question without assuming hostility. Participation must remain voluntary, though, because a quiet resident should not be treated as unfriendly simply for avoiding organised social events.'],
    ['Can online neighbourhood groups make disputes worse?', 'Yes, public complaints can attract speculation and make people defensive before the facts are clear. A photograph may also expose someone unfairly. Groups can still help with information, but moderators should discourage personal accusations and direct sensitive disputes towards private discussion or a proper reporting process.'],
  ]),
  pack('Experience and qualifications in recruitment', 7, [
    ['Why do some employers prefer formal qualifications?', 'Qualifications offer a recognisable indication of structured learning, especially when many applicants have unfamiliar backgrounds. They can also establish essential knowledge for regulated work. But a certificate should not become a shortcut that excludes capable people where the actual task can be assessed directly or learned through supervised experience.'],
    ['When is practical experience more useful than a certificate?', 'It is particularly useful when work requires judgement developed through repeated situations, such as handling customers or diagnosing a fault. An applicant can demonstrate how they approached a real problem. Employers still need to verify the quality of that experience, because years in a role do not automatically mean someone used sound methods.'],
    ['How can an employer assess applicants fairly?', 'The assessment should reflect the work and use clear criteria applied to everyone. A short realistic task can reveal skills better than an informal conversation alone. It should not require applicants to perform substantial unpaid work, and accommodations are important so that the process measures competence rather than unrelated barriers.'],
    ['Should workers continue gaining qualifications after entering a career?', 'Further study can help when knowledge changes or a person wants different responsibilities. However, collecting certificates without applying the learning may add little value. Employers can support targeted courses and practical projects, while recognising that experienced workers may also learn effectively through mentoring and careful reflection on their work.'],
    ['Can demanding degrees for every vacancy create social inequality?', 'Yes, because access to a degree depends partly on money and circumstances, not only ability. If the role does not need that qualification, the requirement can exclude good applicants unnecessarily. Clear skills based selection widens access, while essential professional standards should remain where they genuinely protect quality or safety.'],
  ]),
  pack('Families discussing money with children', 3, [
    ['Why should children learn about ordinary household costs?', 'It helps them understand that money has to cover different needs. A child might think a small purchase is unimportant, but several purchases can use the same money needed for something else. The explanation should be suitable for their age, without making them feel responsible for the family’s financial problems.'],
    ['Is giving children pocket money a useful lesson?', 'It can help them practise making choices with a small amount. They may decide to save for a book instead of buying a snack each day. But families have different budgets, so children should not be judged by how much they receive or expected to learn only through spending money.'],
    ['How can adults teach saving without making children afraid to spend?', 'They can explain that saving is for a purpose, not that every purchase is wrong. A child can keep some money for later and use some for a small enjoyable thing. Adults should also explain that useful spending, such as buying warm clothes, is different from spending because of pressure from friends.'],
    ['Should parents explain why they sometimes refuse a purchase?', 'Yes, a clear reason is better than leaving the child to guess. It might not fit the budget, or the item may not be useful for long. Parents do not need to reveal every private detail, but explaining the choice can help children understand limits without feeling that they have done something wrong.'],
    ['Can advertisements make money lessons more difficult?', 'They can make a product seem necessary or suggest that everyone else owns it. Children may then ask repeatedly without understanding the cost. Adults can discuss how an advertisement is trying to persuade them, using a simple example, rather than only saying no to the product each time.'],
  ]),
  pack('Priorities in ocean and space research', 8, [
    ['How should governments choose between different scientific projects?', 'They should consider likely public benefit, feasibility and the value of knowledge that may not produce an immediate commercial return. Independent review helps test claims. A portfolio is usually wiser than funding only fashionable projects, because uncertainty means that the most visible proposal is not necessarily the one with the greatest long term value.'],
    ['Why does ocean research matter to people far from the coast?', 'Oceans influence climate, food systems and transport, so their condition affects inland communities as well. Better knowledge of marine changes can improve planning and reveal risks that are not locally visible. The connection should be explained concretely, rather than assuming the public will support a project simply because it is scientific.'],
    ['What practical benefits can space research provide?', 'Satellite observation supports weather forecasting, navigation and disaster monitoring, among other uses. Research can also improve engineering methods. However, these benefits do not automatically justify every proposed mission; each project still needs scrutiny of cost, alternatives and what it is expected to contribute beyond a general association with technological progress.'],
    ['Should scientific funding depend heavily on immediate applications?', 'That would neglect research whose value becomes clear only later. Fundamental knowledge can support several future discoveries, but this is not a reason to avoid accountability. Researchers should explain the question, methods and uncertainty, while funders maintain a balance between urgent applied problems and work that expands understanding over a longer horizon.'],
    ['How can scientists communicate an uncertain finding responsibly?', 'They can distinguish observation from interpretation and explain what evidence would change the conclusion. A clear analogy may help, but it should not erase important limitations. Saying that a result is promising rather than guaranteed protects trust, especially when a headline is likely to travel farther than the detailed research paper.'],
  ]),
  pack('Food labels and informed choices', 5, [
    ['What information is most useful on a food label?', 'Ingredients, allergy warnings and clear nutritional information help people compare products and avoid risks. Portion details also matter, because a figure can look small when it refers to an unrealistic serving. The label should be readable in an ordinary shop, not require a long calculation or specialist knowledge before making a choice.'],
    ['Why can claims on food packaging be confusing?', 'A package may highlight one attractive feature, such as low fat, while the product contains a large amount of sugar. The claim might be technically true but create a misleading overall impression. Consistent rules and plain explanations would help buyers consider the whole product rather than focus on one promotional phrase.'],
    ['Should restaurants provide nutritional information about meals?', 'For standard meals, useful information can help people with particular health needs make choices. Small restaurants may need practical support to provide accurate figures, though. Estimates should be clearly identified, and information should supplement good food rather than turn every meal into a precise calculation that gives customers false certainty.'],
    ['How can children learn to understand food packaging?', 'They can compare familiar products and discuss the difference between a serving and the whole packet. That makes the information concrete. Lessons should avoid dividing every food into a simple good or bad category, because overall habits and portions matter, and children may have limited control over what their family can afford.'],
    ['Are labels enough to improve eating habits?', 'No, because people also need affordable choices, time and suitable places to prepare food. A clear label cannot make a healthier option available in a shop that does not stock it. Information is important, but it should be combined with practical improvements rather than treating every dietary problem as ignorance.'],
  ]),
  pack('Street design and road safety', 6.5, [
    ['Why is street design important for safe driving?', 'The shape of a road influences speed and attention before a driver reads a sign. A narrow crossing or raised junction can encourage caution where people walk. Education still matters, but design can reduce reliance on every driver making the perfect decision at every moment, which is a more realistic approach.'],
    ['Should streets near schools have different traffic rules?', 'Yes, children need additional protection because they may misjudge speed and are less visible behind parked cars. Timed restrictions and clear crossings can help. The arrangements should still allow access for disabled pupils and essential services, so safety measures need planning rather than simply closing a road without alternatives.'],
    ['How can a city encourage safer cycling?', 'Connected routes that separate cyclists from fast traffic can make a substantial difference. A short protected section is less useful if it ends at a dangerous junction. Secure parking and clear priority rules also help, while training supports confidence. The goal should be a complete usable journey, not a collection of impressive isolated projects.'],
    ['Is stricter punishment the best way to reduce road accidents?', 'It can discourage deliberate dangerous behaviour when enforcement is consistent, but it does not solve poor visibility or confusing layouts. Some risks need engineering changes and better training. Authorities should examine the actual causes of collisions rather than assume that one stronger penalty will address every type of mistake.'],
    ['What should transport planners learn from pedestrians?', 'Pedestrians can explain where routes are inconvenient, where waiting times encourage risky crossings and where mobility barriers occur. An older resident may notice that a signal changes too quickly. This practical evidence complements traffic models and helps planners judge access as a human experience, rather than only count vehicles moving through a junction.'],
  ]),
  pack('Keeping small towns economically viable', 7, [
    ['Why do some small towns struggle to retain young adults?', 'Study and specialised work are often concentrated elsewhere, so leaving can be a practical necessity. Returning may be difficult if suitable housing and careers are limited. A town should not interpret every departure as disloyalty; it needs to understand which opportunities people require to build an adult life there.'],
    ['Can remote employment help smaller communities?', 'It can bring income without requiring a large local employer, provided connectivity and services are dependable. New residents may support shops and schools. However, grants that attract people without addressing housing pressure can disadvantage existing residents, so the benefits need to be shared through infrastructure and thoughtful local planning.'],
    ['What kinds of business support are useful outside major cities?', 'Reliable transport, digital connections and access to advice can be more valuable than a brief publicity campaign. Small firms may need shared facilities or help finding customers beyond the local market. Support should respond to actual barriers and avoid funding businesses only because they fit an appealing image of rural life.'],
    ['Should every town aim to attract large industrial employers?', 'No, one large employer can create jobs but also make the community vulnerable if it closes. A range of smaller activities may provide greater resilience. The suitable mix depends on skills, land and transport, and residents should consider environmental and service costs alongside the number of promised vacancies.'],
    ['How can local authorities measure successful regional development?', 'They should look beyond the number of people arriving. Stable employment, affordable housing and access to services show whether daily life is improving for existing residents too. A rising population can hide pressure on schools or rents, so several measures are needed rather than a single headline figure.'],
  ]),
  pack('Preserving personal photographs and digital records', 5.5, [
    ['Why do people keep large collections of digital photographs?', 'Taking a picture is easy, and storage makes it possible to keep moments that once might not have been recorded. Families may value ordinary scenes as well as special events. The difficulty is that quantity can make the important images hard to find unless the collection is organised occasionally.'],
    ['Is online storage a reliable way to preserve memories?', 'It is useful, especially if a phone is lost, but it should not be the only copy. Accounts can become inaccessible and services can change. Keeping another copy in a common format gives a family more control, rather than assuming that automatic upload guarantees permanent access to every important photograph.'],
    ['Should families consider privacy when sharing photographs?', 'Yes, because a picture may reveal a child’s routine, location or personal information. People shown in it should have a say where possible. A private album can preserve connection without making everything public, and families should review settings rather than assume that an old choice still provides the protection they expect.'],
    ['How can future generations understand a digital family archive?', 'Names, dates and short explanations make images meaningful after the people who recognise them are gone. Otherwise, a large folder can become a collection of unidentified faces. Families should also keep files in usable formats and arrange access appropriately, balancing preservation with the privacy of the people described.'],
    ['Can keeping every record make remembering more difficult?', 'Yes, if the volume prevents people from revisiting what matters. Choosing a small album can encourage reflection and conversation in a way that thousands of unsorted files do not. Selection should be thoughtful, though; a record that seems ordinary now may become valuable later, so organisation is safer than indiscriminate deletion.'],
  ]),
  pack('Weather information and everyday planning', 4.5, [
    ['Why do people sometimes expect weather forecasts to be exact?', 'A phone often shows one simple symbol or number, which can look like a promise. But conditions vary across places and predictions have uncertainty. Explaining the chance of rain helps people make choices, while a confident looking display without context may encourage unfair expectations of a forecast.'],
    ['How can better forecasts help small businesses?', 'A farmer can plan work, and an outdoor café can prepare staff or protect equipment. Advance information gives them time to make adjustments rather than react after a problem starts. They still need a backup plan, because no forecast can remove every risk or guarantee the same conditions at each location.'],
    ['Should outdoor events always be cancelled when rain is possible?', 'No, it depends on the likely severity and the activity. Light rain may be manageable with shelter, while lightning or strong winds can make the event unsafe. Organisers need clear safety thresholds and a way to inform visitors, rather than decide only according to ticket income or a vague forecast symbol.'],
    ['Why should warning messages use simple language?', 'People need to understand what to do quickly, including visitors and those unfamiliar with technical terms. A warning that explains the area, timing and action is more useful than a dramatic phrase alone. Different channels also matter, since relying only on an app may miss people without a working phone.'],
    ['Can schools use weather information to teach practical skills?', 'Yes, pupils can compare forecasts with observations and discuss why differences occur. That teaches them to use evidence without expecting perfect certainty. They can also plan a safe outdoor activity, learning that a forecast supports judgement rather than replaces the need to consider local conditions and sensible precautions.'],
  ]),
  pack('Inclusive participation in school sport', 3.5, [
    ['Why should schools offer more than one kind of sport?', 'Children have different interests and abilities. Someone who dislikes a competitive ball game may enjoy swimming or walking. Offering choices helps more pupils stay active, rather than making sport seem suitable only for the strongest players. Schools still need enough time and space to teach each activity safely.'],
    ['Should every pupil be expected to compete against others?', 'Not in every activity. Competition can be exciting and teach teamwork, but some children feel discouraged when they always lose. Personal goals, such as improving balance or completing a longer walk, can also show progress. A school can offer both approaches instead of treating competition as the only reason to exercise.'],
    ['How can teachers help pupils who feel embarrassed during sport?', 'They can avoid public comments about bodies or ability and begin with manageable tasks. Small groups and supportive feedback may make practice less frightening. The teacher should also stop teasing quickly, because a child is unlikely to improve confidence in an environment where classmates are allowed to make mistakes humiliating.'],
    ['Is expensive equipment necessary for good school sport?', 'Usually not for a useful basic programme. Simple equipment and well planned activities can develop movement and cooperation. Some sports do require specialist facilities, but schools should not make participation depend on a family buying costly items. Shared equipment helps children take part without displaying differences in income.'],
    ['How can school sport encourage activity after pupils leave school?', 'It should help pupils find activities they can continue and feel comfortable doing. Learning only one sport with expensive facilities may limit future choices. Walking, simple fitness routines and local clubs provide different routes, while a positive experience matters more than making every pupil reach the same competitive standard.'],
  ]),
  pack('Fairness in examinations and grading', 7.5, [
    ['What makes an examination fair?', 'It should test the intended knowledge and skills with clear instructions and reasonable conditions. Fairness does not mean every candidate receives an easy question. It means irrelevant barriers, such as an inaccessible format, do not determine the result, and the marking process applies consistent criteria to comparable answers.'],
    ['Should one examination determine a student’s final grade?', 'I would usually prefer several sources of evidence, because illness or an unusual problem can affect one day. However, continuous assessment can create constant pressure if badly organised. A balanced system should sample achievement reliably while keeping the workload manageable, rather than simply replace one high stakes test with dozens of smaller ones.'],
    ['How useful are practical tasks in assessing learning?', 'They can show whether a student applies knowledge to a real problem, which a short written answer may miss. The task needs clear criteria and sufficient observation, especially if group work is involved. Otherwise, a polished result may hide unequal contributions or outside assistance, so process evidence remains important.'],
    ['Can publishing school rankings improve education?', 'Rankings can draw attention to outcomes, but they may simplify differences in intake and resources. Schools might focus narrowly on the measured result or discourage pupils who could lower it. Public information should explain context and broader indicators, so accountability does not become a competition based on an incomplete picture.'],
    ['How should students receive feedback on a weak result?', 'Feedback should identify the specific gap and offer a feasible next step. A low score alone tells a student little about how to improve. They also need an opportunity to practise again, because explaining a mistake without time to address it can leave the assessment feeling like a final judgement rather than part of learning.'],
  ]),
  pack('Accessible design for everyday products', 6.5, [
    ['Why should designers consider users with disabilities from the beginning?', 'Changing a finished product can be expensive and may produce an awkward solution. Early consultation reveals needs that a designer might not anticipate, such as a control that requires fine finger movement. Considering those needs can improve usability for many people, including someone with a temporary injury or limited vision.'],
    ['Does accessible design usually make a product less attractive?', 'Not necessarily. Clear controls, readable information and comfortable handles can be integrated into an attractive design. The idea that accessibility must look separate often reflects poor planning. Some compromises are unavoidable, but testing with different users is better than assuming that appearance and practical access always compete.'],
    ['What can companies learn from observing real users?', 'They can see workarounds and points of difficulty that a questionnaire may not reveal. A person might repeatedly miss a small button but hesitate to describe that as a design fault. Observation should be respectful and consensual, and the findings need to influence decisions rather than become a demonstration of consultation only.'],
    ['Should public purchasing favour accessible products?', 'Yes, public services serve a wide range of people and can create demand for better designs. Requirements must be specific and tested, not satisfied by a vague claim on packaging. The purchaser should also consider maintenance and training, because an accessible device offers little benefit if staff cannot use or support it properly.'],
    ['Can simpler technology be better than adding more features?', 'Certainly, if extra functions make the essential task confusing. A person buying a heating control may need a clear temperature setting more than several complex modes. Designers should identify the main purpose and make it dependable, while allowing optional features without forcing every user through an unnecessarily complicated interface.'],
  ]),
  pack('Film stories and cultural representation', 6, [
    ['How can films influence the way people see another country?', 'A film provides memorable characters and settings, which viewers may mistake for a complete picture. Repeated stereotypes can become familiar assumptions even when the story is fictional. A range of voices and contexts helps, but audiences also need to recognise that one entertaining narrative cannot represent everyone in a society.'],
    ['Why might local film industries need support?', 'Smaller markets may struggle to fund stories in a local language or compete for cinema space. Support can preserve opportunities for different experiences to be portrayed. It should still use transparent criteria and protect creative independence, rather than reward only stories that present an officially preferred image of the country.'],
    ['Do subtitles help audiences engage with other cultures?', 'They make a wider range of films accessible without replacing the original voices, which can preserve tone and character. Some viewers find reading distracting, so good timing and translation matter. Availability also matters; subtitles alone cannot help if a platform never offers films from less commercially prominent places.'],
    ['Should filmmakers avoid difficult or controversial subjects?', 'No, films can encourage discussion about experiences people rarely acknowledge. Responsibility lies in research and thoughtful portrayal, not in removing every uncomfortable idea. The context and intended audience matter, especially for material involving violence or trauma, and clear information allows viewers to make an informed choice about watching.'],
    ['Can entertainment and education coexist in a film?', 'Yes, an engaging story can make viewers curious about a period or issue they previously ignored. But a film often simplifies events to create a clear narrative. That is why it is useful to distinguish dramatic interpretation from established evidence, rather than treat an enjoyable historical film as a complete factual account.'],
  ]),
  pack('Borrowing and consumer credit', 5, [
    ['Why do people use credit for everyday purchases?', 'It can bridge a temporary gap or make a large necessary purchase possible. Advertising also presents delayed payment as painless, which can hide the total obligation. The reason matters: borrowing for an essential appliance is different from repeatedly buying things mainly because the payment is postponed.'],
    ['What information should lenders make clear?', 'People need the total cost, payment dates and what happens if they miss a payment. A small monthly amount can look affordable while continuing for a long time. Clear examples are useful, and important terms should not be hidden in a long document that appears only after the customer has decided to buy.'],
    ['Should borrowing services check whether customers can repay?', 'Yes, because easy approval can create serious harm when repayments are unrealistic. Checks should be proportionate and respect privacy, but a company should not profit from lending that it can reasonably expect a customer to struggle with. Customers also need honest information and a way to obtain help early.'],
    ['How can people avoid taking on too many small debts?', 'They can keep a single record of all commitments and compare the total with reliable income. Separate instalment plans may each seem minor, while together they leave little money for essentials. Planning helps, but wider support is needed when borrowing reflects insufficient income rather than simply poor organisation.'],
    ['Why can debt problems be difficult to discuss?', 'People may feel ashamed or worry that asking for help will reveal failure to family members. That delay can allow fees and stress to grow. Confidential, impartial advice gives a safer first step, and lenders should explain assistance clearly rather than communicate only through threatening reminders once a payment is missed.'],
  ]),
];
